# IMPERIA COLD WAR — Historical negotiating references and complete treaty-editor help
from treaties import TREATIES

HISTORY={
 1:('SALT I, 1972','The Interim Agreement froze strategic missile launchers for five years. This proposal adapts that principle to stored nuclear-capable ICBMs: no initial destruction, a ceiling at each signatory\'s agreed reserve total, and a five-year term. Launchers, deployed formations and warhead counts are outside this reserve agreement.'),
 2:('ABM Treaty, 1972','The historical treaty limited strategic anti-ballistic-missile deployment sites. Ordinary air-defence missiles are not equivalent to those installations. This proposal opens strategic-defence consultations without destroying missiles or claiming a site limit. Add only the actual reserve, economic or regional commitments both governments wish to negotiate.'),
 3:('SALT II, 1979','The historical agreement addressed strategic delivery-vehicle ceilings and was signed but not ratified. This proposal offers a five-year freeze of stored nuclear ICBM reserves, without immediate destruction. That is a reserve-based adaptation, not the historical launcher and bomber totals. Both players may ratify their own agreed paper.'),
 4:('INF Treaty, 1987','The historical treaty eliminated ground-launched ballistic and cruise missiles with ranges from 500 to 5,500 km, including nuclear and conventional systems. This proposal selects the loaded missile models in that range, retires 100% of their stored reserves on signature, and keeps a zero reserve ceiling until withdrawal. Deployed systems and launchers are outside the reserve count.'),
 5:('START I, 1991','The historical treaty limited deployed strategic delivery vehicles and attributed warheads. This proposal uses a 25% reduction of stored nuclear ICBMs and a continuing ceiling on the remaining reserves, with a 15-year term. The percentage is a gameplay adaptation, not the historical 1,600 delivery-vehicle or 6,000 warhead ceiling. Review the actual missile quantities before sending.'),
 6:('Reykjavik proposals, 1986','The summit explored deep strategic reductions but did not conclude this framework. This proposal adapts the 50% reduction discussed there to stored nuclear ICBMs, with a ceiling on the remaining reserves. Concluding it is an alternative historical choice requiring both governments\' signatures.'),
 7:('Nuclear risk reduction','A standing consultation channel. It destroys no missiles by default. You may negotiate additional missile, commercial, investment or regional terms before sending the paper.'),
 8:('Commercial cooperation','Suggested opening terms improve bilateral trade opinion by 100 and relations by 25. This is access and diplomatic cooperation, not guaranteed resource deliveries or cash. Sanctions relief and other concessions remain separate choices.'),
 9:('Investment cooperation','Suggested opening terms reduce normal bilateral investment cost and construction duration by 5%. Projects still follow their ordinary approval and construction requirements. The scientific programme is an additional explicitly funded clause when its American initiative has been completed.'),
 10:('Regional stand-down','Each government names the recipient of its own military missions. The suggested paper recalls both attaches and volunteers. A deployed volunteer recall costs its sending government 50 Political Power and 15 Command Power and needs the normal recall permission. Local governments continue their own wars.'),
 11:('Sanctions and restraint','Suggested opening terms lift the four named Imperia sanction classes between Washington and Moscow. Each existing class actually lifted costs its imposing government 35 Political Power. Ordinary embargoes remain separate. Review both governments\' real concessions before sending.'),
 12:('Your own agreement','Start with an empty negotiating paper. Choose the covered missile families, reduction, reserve ceiling, sanctions, trade, investment, term, missions and optional participants. No missiles, money or spirits change when the draft opens. The other government must review and sign the complete proposal.')}


def build(loc,defined):
    scripts=[]
    loc('icwt_workflow_help','§YTreaty maker§!\n1. Choose a historical or suggested proposal, or open Custom agreement.\n2. Click a setting to cycle its values. Select mission recipients and optional signatories on the Participants page.\n3. Review complete terms and Prospective effects, then submit the paper.\n4. The other core government reviews that exact paper and signs, declines or proposes revised terms.\n5. Only acceptance applies the actual missile reductions, fees and spirits.\n\nReserve ceilings are inspected weekly. Exceeding a ceiling pauses the associated continuing benefits. Restore the ceiling through its decision or negotiate a different agreement. Completed destruction and payments are not refunded by withdrawal. Programme-only addenda leave the original material commitments unchanged.')
    descriptions={
 'scope':('Covered missiles','Click to cycle: none, nuclear ICBMs, ballistic missiles, cruise missiles, all listed reserves, all nuclear-capable reserves, or INF-range reserves. Nuclear-capable means the loaded ICBM and nuclear ballistic equipment families. The ordinary ballistic and cruise families are conventional. Only stored equipment is counted; deployed formations, launchers and separate warheads are excluded.'),
 'reduction':('Missile reduction','Click to cycle 0%, 10%, 25%, 50% or 100%. The selected share of each covered reserve model is dismantled once, rounded to whole missiles. Exact American and Soviet quantities are fixed when the proposal is sent; an additional signatory fixes its own quantities on review. A revised proposal needs a new review. 0% means no immediate destruction and may still accompany a reserve freeze.'),
 'reserve_limit':('Reserve ceiling','No continuing ceiling leaves only the one-time reduction. Cap post-reduction reserves freezes the total covered reserves at their agreed remaining quantity. You may replace models within that total. Weekly inspections pause linked programme benefits, trade and investment privileges, joint tension relief and the selected sanctions protection if the ceiling is exceeded. Excess equipment is not destroyed automatically. Use Restore the missile ceiling in Decisions to dismantle only the excess; it does not charge another setup fee.'),
 'sanctions':('Core sanctions relief','Choose none, one named diplomatic, financial, economic or military class, or all four. Only actual existing sanctions between the USA and Soviet Union are lifted. Each lifted class costs its imposing government 35 Political Power. Both mirrors are removed. Reimposition of the selected classes is blocked while the two governments jointly observe the agreement. A broken missile ceiling suspends that protection at the next inspection. Native embargoes are a separate diplomatic action.'),
 'trade':('Trade protocol','Choose none, trade opinion +50 and relations +10, or trade opinion +100 and relations +25. The rate applies between actual participating governments at peace and observing the agreement. It improves commercial relations; it does not create resource shipments, treasury income or free factories. Overlapping commercial agreements use the strongest applicable benefit rather than stacking copies.'),
 'investment':('Investment protocol','Choose none, cost and duration -5%, or cost and duration -10%. These are bilateral discounts on ordinary native investment projects between participating governments observing the agreement. Projects still need their usual approval and construction requirements. Existing applicable discounts are respected. This is not a global construction-speed bonus or an automatic building grant.'),
 'duration':('Agreement term','Choose until withdrawal, or 180, 365, 730, 1,825 or 5,475 days from signature. Each participant may withdraw earlier. Expiry or departure ends the relevant ongoing benefits and payments; completed missile destruction and fees remain completed. An addendum changes the joint programme only and retains the original treaty end date.'),
 'regional':('Military missions','Choose none, recall own attaches, recall own volunteers, or both. On Participants, each core government selects its own mission recipient. A deployed volunteer recall requires the normal game rule, 50 Political Power and 15 Command Power. The local government keeps control of its war. A funded regional programme also requires the selected missions to remain absent; sending them back pauses that programme.'),
 'programme':('Joint programme','Optional bilateral scientific, civilian supply, strategic consultation or regional cooperation programmes are unlocked by Bilateral Cooperation, Grain and Credit, Arms-Control Talks and Linkage and Restraint respectively. They attach to the corresponding investment, commercial, SALT I or regional instrument, not the blank custom instrument. Both governments approve their complete spirit modifiers and GDP-based funding in Prospective effects. Optional third countries do not receive this bilateral programme. War, embargoes, sanctions, ended joint participation or broken agreed missile ceilings pause its benefits and weekly funding without repeating setup charges on restoration.'),
 'usa_target':('American mission recipient','Click, then select the country from which the United States will recall its own selected military missions. This is the recipient of American attaches or volunteers, not the Soviet recipient. The final paper names both targets and previews the actual recall costs. Another country\'s war is not ended.'),
 'sov_target':('Soviet mission recipient','Click, then select the country from which the Soviet Union will recall its own selected military missions. This is the recipient of Soviet attaches or volunteers, not the American recipient. Each core government is responsible for its own commitments.'),
 'invites':('Additional signatories','Up to six additional governments may be invited alongside the two core parties. Each reviews and decides for itself. An unanswered invitation does not delay the American-Soviet signature. Acceptance before conclusion waits for the core agreement; acceptance afterwards is accession. Each government contributes only its own approved obligations. The bilateral joint programme is reserved to the two core governments.'),
 'pending':('Pending negotiating paper','Only one core paper awaits signature at a time. Open it to review its actual current terms. The author cannot provide the other government\'s signature. Revised terms invalidate the previous review. Preparing a different draft never overwrites a pending proposal or changes a standing agreement.')}
    descriptions['signature']=('Signature and revised terms','The other core government must review this exact paper before signing. Check Prospective effects for both governments\' missile quantities, fees and benefits. A revised paper needs a fresh review. Older unsigned papers based on the superseded production clause need revised missile terms before signature. Select a real reserve reduction or ceiling, then send the revised paper for the other government\'s approval. Programme-only addenda preserve the original material terms.')
    for field,(title,body) in descriptions.items():
        current='\n§YSelected:§! [icwt_value_'+field+']' if field in ('scope','reduction','reserve_limit','sanctions','trade','investment','duration','regional','programme') else ''
        loc('icwt_help_'+field,'§Y'+title+'§!'+current+'\n\n'+body)
    for n,(title,body) in HISTORY.items():loc(f'icwt_history_{n}','§Y'+title+'§!\n\n'+body+'\n\nOpening a proposal does not send or sign it. All shown terms remain negotiable before submission.')
    rows=[]
    for n in range(1,len(TREATIES)+1):
        rows.append((f'check_variable = {{ icwt_template = {n} }} has_country_flag = icwt_member_{n} has_country_flag = icwt_limit_broken_{n}','icwt_cap_status_bad'))
        rows.append((f'check_variable = {{ icwt_template = {n} }} has_country_flag = icwt_member_{n} check_variable = {{ global.icwt_{n}_reserve_limit = 1 }}','icwt_cap_status_good'))
    scripts.append(defined('icwt_limit_status',rows))
    return scripts
